%N A135978 Primes p such that 2^p-1 has exactly 2 prime factors.
%C A135978 a(40) >= 1277. - _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 29 2018
%C A135978 Semiprimes 2^p-1 are good candidates for composite Mersenne numbers which together with their smallest (and hence all) prime factors lie between two consecutive Mersenne primes (A000668). Although an example is not yet known as of today, consecutive Mersenne prime exponents p(n) = A000043(n) are regularly more than a factor 2 apart: for n = 12, 14, 20, 31, 35, 37, ..., p(n+1)/p(n) ~ 4.1, 2.1, 2.2, 3.5, 2.1, 2.3, .... In that case, one might have 2^p(n) < P < Q < P*Q = 2^k-1 < 2^p(n+1)-1. - _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 05 2026
%H A135978 Jason Dong, in reply to Tomasz Ordowski, <a href="https://groups.google.com/g/seqfan/c/N5zt97Tm6eg/m/G3GdAtulAQAJ">The Mersenne primes as the only sequence records of LPF(2^n - 1)</a>, SeqFan group, Sep 2026.
%t A135978 k = {}; Do[If[ ! PrimeQ[2^Prime[n] - 1], c = FactorInteger[2^Prime[n] - 1]; d = Length[c]; If[d == 2, AppendTo[k, Prime[n]]]], {n, 1, 40}]; k
%o A135978 (PARI) select( {is_A135978(p)=bigomega(2^p-1)==2&&isprime(p)}, [1..200]) \\ _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 05 2026
